      - In both 2D and 3D C versions of build_node, the split median target variable m and
        coordinate bounds tmp were declared as standard int (32-bit signed). Similarly, the
        parameter k in the partition function sel_k was declared as int k.
      - Because your star coordinates are projected in Cartesian space and scaled by 10⁹ to
        preserve sub-milliparsec accuracy, coordinate values easily range from 2 × 10¹¹ to 9 × 10¹²
        parsecs. This is far larger than the maximum value a 32-bit signed integer can hold
        (2,147,483,647).
      - Consequently, the coordinates truncated and wrapped around to extremely small/negative
        numbers. Since the target split point m was truncated, sel_k always selected the element
        with the absolute minimum coordinate as the median, which put all other $N-1$ elements in
        the hi child.
      - This converted the O(N log N) balanced construction into an O(N²) linked list of recursion
        depth equal to the number of stars (e.g., 160,000+ deep), causing the program to hang for
        minutes before exceeding the 8MB Linux stack limit and crashing with a segmentation fault.

   2. Un-averaged Root mean Bug:
      - In kd_build, the library was loading items and retrieving the raw sum of coordinates inside
        mean. However, it passed this raw sum directly to build_node for level 1 instead of
        dividing it by item_count.
      - This meant the root node split point was $159,967$ times larger than the actual average,
        further exacerbating the imbalanced split.

