  Raylib 3D Star Catalog Viewer Implementation Plan

  1. Background & Motivation
  With over ~350 million Gaia stars now efficiently indexed into high-performance memory-mapped
  .kdtree files, we need a high-performance 3D visualization tool. The goal is to build a "real" C
  viewer using the raylib framework (compiled at /home/murf/external-sources/raylib). The viewer
  will automatically load the .kdtree files, position the camera at Earth (0, 0, 0), and orient the
  view toward Polaris (which aligns with the +Z axis in our celestial coordinate mapping) for an
  authentic astronomical experience.

  2. Scope & Impact
   * Target Application: A standalone C application (viewer.c).
   * Graphics Backend: Raylib (OpenGL 3.3+ for instancing support).
   * Data Source: Multiple .kdtree files located in /backup/star-catalogs/.
   * Performance Target: Maintain 60 FPS while rendering tens to hundreds of millions of stars.

  3. Proposed Solution
  Based on our consultation, we will implement GPU Instancing and Float32 Parsec Scaling:
   * GPU Instancing: Instead of CPU-bound rendering loops, we will load the star coordinates
     directly into a massive OpenGL Vertex Buffer Object (VBO). Raylib's rlgl abstraction layer
     (rlLoadVertexBuffer, rlEnableInstancedVertexArray) will be used alongside a custom
     vertex/fragment shader to draw millions of star instances natively on the GPU.
   * Float32 Parsec Scaling: The .kdtree 64-bit integer coordinates (scaled by 10⁹) will be divided
     by 10⁹ during the load phase to yield 32-bit floating-point coordinates in standard Parsecs
     (1.0 = 1 pc). This provides excellent precision for deep-space visualization.
   * Camera Orientation: A Camera3D will be initialized with position = {0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f} and
     target = {0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f}. Since Polaris has a declination of ~+89°, it lies almost
     perfectly along the +Z axis.
   * Memory Mapping: We will reuse the kd_mmap_node structure to rapidly map and read the binary
     .kdtree files.

  4. Alternatives Considered
   * KD-Tree View Frustum Culling: We considered dynamically querying the KD-trees every frame to
     only upload visible stars to the GPU. This was rejected because scanning millions of nodes on
     the CPU every frame introduces jitter, whereas modern GPUs can trivially process vertex
     instancing for hundreds of millions of points.
   * Kiloparsec Scaling: We considered dividing coordinates by 10¹² to render in kiloparsecs, but
     standard Parsecs provide better float precision for relatively nearby star systems.

  5. Phased Implementation Plan
   1. Phase 1: Project Setup & Dependencies
       * Create viewer.c and a Makefile configured to link against
         /home/murf/external-sources/raylib/src/libraylib.a, OpenGL, lm, and our local libkdtree.a.
   2. Phase 2: KD-Tree File Loader
       * Implement a routine using glob or dirent to automatically find all
         GaiaSource_Filtered_*.kdtree files in /backup/star-catalogs/.
       * Iterate through each file, memory-map it, and extract the (x, y, z) coordinates into a
         massive contiguous float array.
   3. Phase 3: Instanced Shader Development
       * Write a basic star.vs (Vertex Shader) to handle MVP transformations and gl_InstanceID
         positioning.
       * Write star.fs (Fragment Shader) to output a simple white/yellow point or textured
         billboard.
   4. Phase 4: Raylib Camera & Core Loop
       * Initialize the Raylib window (InitWindow).
       * Setup the Camera3D facing Polaris (0,0,1).
       * Bind the coordinate array to a VBO and invoke rlDrawMeshInstanced() inside the
         BeginDrawing() loop.
   5. Phase 5: Controls & UI
       * Enable UpdateCamera(&camera, CAMERA_FREE) to allow the user to fly around the galaxy.
       * Draw an FPS counter and star count to the HUD.

  6. Verification
   * Compile the project successfully without linker errors.
   * Launch the viewer and verify that it starts instantaneously (thanks to mmap).
   * Confirm the camera starts at (0,0,0) and looks toward a dense cluster of stars on the +Z axis
